From 090a662a3b43babf41ca619386347b0fa37772b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tommy <tommy@pham.cc>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 04:40:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 41/42] Rollback: the IOP in the snapshot, SPU2 output muted in
 re-simulated frames

ps2xIOP: IopSubsystem::saveState/loadState(IopSnapshot) copy the emulated IOP by value: IOP RAM and its
ownership map, scratch, hardware registers, allocations, DMA start; the kernel (threads with CPU states,
semaphores, event flags, ready/delay queues relinked by thread id); cdvd drive state; RPC servers; intrman;
timrman; ioman file positions; import registry; the SPU2 (sample RAM, voices, reverb, input streams, IRQ,
clock); the emulator's scalars, modules, pending DMA interrupts and guest callbacks, SIF command handlers,
the EE->IOP SIF command queue and the SIF software registers. Host resources (disc image handles, the ISO
index, the host audio queue) stay as they are. hashState for the netplay desync check.
SoundEngine::setOutputMuted: the mix runs, nothing reaches the host output.

ps2_rollback: setIncludeIop(true) puts the IOP into every capture/restore (default off: phase 2's frozen
IOP and gate are unchanged); hashIop; setIopOutputMuted. PS2X_ROLLBACK_IOP_STATE tells game modules.

Netplay needs it (Tekken 4 phase 3): with the IOP outside the state, a peer whose live frames ran on a
predicted input fed those frames' sound commands to its IOP, the replies differed from the other peer's
(page 0x1cca000, EE cycle +8). Rolled back with the EE, the IOP only ever keeps the timeline that sticks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
